---
kind: scanrepo
ext: .scanrepo
version: 1
mime: application/x-scanrepo
binary: false
trigger: scanrepo paper git printed repo physical software scan back
---

## Purpose

Printable master of a git snapshot. A human can read the source on the page. A phone camera recovers the same bytes through page QR codes, then a restorer rebuilds blobs, a tree, and a commit so `git checkout` matches the paper.

## Structure

1. First line is the magic banner `# scanrepo v1`
2. YAML frontmatter between `---` fences with keys: repo, edition, branch, commit, tree, parent, pages
3. One or more page blocks. Each block starts with `## PAGE <n>` and ends with `## END PAGE`
4. Inside a page block, header lines are `key: value` until a line that is exactly `text:`
5. Lines after `text:` and before `## END PAGE` are the file bytes, including blank lines
6. Required page keys: path, mode, blob, chunk, chunk_of
7. Cover identity lives only in frontmatter. File bytes live only in page blocks.

## Parse rules

- Reject the file if line 1 is not `# scanrepo v1`
- Frontmatter keys are required: repo, edition, branch, commit, tree, parent, pages
- `parent` may be the token `none`
- Page blocks must be numbered 1..pages with no gaps
- Split header lines on the first `: `
- File bytes are everything between the `text:` line and `## END PAGE`, with the trailing newline before `## END PAGE` stripped
- Rejoin chunks for the same path in chunk order before hashing
- Blob check: SHA-1 of `blob <len>\0` plus rejoined bytes must equal the `blob` field
- Tree and commit fields are git object ids; the restorer recomputes them and rejects a mismatch
- Trees nest: each directory is its own tree object (mode `40000`). Entries sort by name bytes, with a directory name compared as `name/`
- If a page has `size`, its bytes are exactly `size` bytes after the `text:` line, followed by `\n## END PAGE`. This wins over the line rule
- Ignore keys you do not know

## Write rules

- Always start with `# scanrepo v1`
- One edition per file. A later printing is a new file with edition bumped and parent set to the prior commit
- Never put packfiles or deltas in the body. Store full blob text
- Chunk a file only when it will not fit one printed page (about 1800 source characters). The reference writer uses at most 1800 bytes of UTF-8 and 72 lines per page, splits at line ends, and splits a longer line at a character boundary
- Text means valid UTF-8 with no NUL byte. Anything else is binary and stays off the page
- A symlink (mode 120000) is a page whose text is the link target
- Hash with git blob rules, not raw SHA-1 of the file
- Page order is the recovery order. Path order inside a tree is byte-sorted, git style

## Format 1.1

Optional keys, same banner. A 1.1 master is still a v1 file, and `sample.scanrepo` (1.0) still restores. `format: 1.1` in the frontmatter marks it. Writers emit `size` on every page.

Frontmatter keys that make the commit id checkable:

- `author`, `committer`: the commit's author and committer lines after the keyword, verbatim (`Name <email> <unix-time> <tz>`)
- `message`: the commit message as a JSON string (exact, UTF-8)
- `commit_extra`: JSON string of the header lines after `committer` (`gpgsig`, `encoding`, `mergetag`), each with its newline. Absent when empty
- `parents`: space-separated parent ids, merge commits only. `parent` holds the first
- `<key>_b64`: base64 of the raw bytes, in place of `author`, `committer`, `message` or `commit_extra` when they are not UTF-8
- `commit_object_b64`: base64 of the whole commit object, only when it does not fit the layout below
- `omitted`: line count of the OMITTED block

Commit object, hashed as `commit <len>\0` plus these bytes:

```
tree <tree>
parent <id>          one line per parent, none when parent is none
author <author>
committer <committer>
<commit_extra>
                     blank line
<message>
```

A 1.0 master has none of these keys. The restorer checks blobs and tree and says the commit is not verified.

Files that stay off paper keep their ids, so the tree and commit still verify. They go in an optional block between the frontmatter and page 1:

```
## OMITTED
<mode> <blob-or-commit-id> <reason> <path>
## END OMITTED
```

Reasons: `binary`, `too-large`, `excluded`, `submodule` (mode 160000). The restored checkout lacks these files and git shows them deleted.

## QR payloads

Byte mode with an ECI 26 (UTF-8) header. Error correction M or better, L only when M will not fit. Version 40 at most.

- Cover: `C|scanrepo1|<commit>|<i>/<n>` newline, then segment i. The segments joined in order are the master up to `## PAGE 1`. `n` is 1 unless that text runs past about 1900 bytes (long signatures, many omitted files). Then the cover codes get their own pages
- Page: `P|scanrepo1|<commit>|<page>/<pages>` newline, then the page block from `## PAGE <page>` through `## END PAGE`
- Rebuild: the cover segments, then each page block followed by a newline. That is the master, byte for byte
- Reject a set of codes that carry more than one commit id

## Example

See sample.scanrepo (format 1.0)
